<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paranthropus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as robust australopithecines</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paranthropus is a genus of extinct hominin with three widely accepted species, P. aethiopicus, P. robustus and P. boisei, also called the robust australopithecines; its validity is contested, and it is sometimes considered synonymous with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australopithecus">Australopithecus</a>. They lived between approximately 2.9 and at least 1.2 million years ago, had robust skulls with a gorilla-like sagittal crest and broad grinding teeth, were bipeds and had comparatively small bodies. Robert Broom erected the genus in 1938 from a braincase found at Kromdraai in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and Mary Leakey discovered P. boisei at Olduvai Gorge, Tanzania, in 1959.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paranthropus.jpg" alt="Paranthropus" width="330" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Einsamer Schütze · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smac_Paläolithikum_013.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name comes from the Ancient Greek para, beside, and anthropos, man. The type species, P. robustus, rests on a male braincase found at Kromdraai, about 70 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, by the schoolboy Gert Terblanche. Louis Leakey named the Olduvai skull OH 5 Zinjanthropus boisei, after the benefactor Charles Watson Boise, and it was later placed in Paranthropus as more specimens came to light. P. aethiopicus was assigned to the genus in 1986 after the skull KNM WT 17000 was found. Whether the genus is valid remains debated: in 1951 Sherwood Washburn and Bruce Patterson suggested it was a junior synonym of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australopithecus">Australopithecus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>P. aethiopicus is the earliest species, its oldest remains, from the Omo Kibish Formation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, being dated to 2.6 million years ago. The oldest P. boisei remains, from Malema in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>, date to about 2.3 million years ago, and the species changed remarkably little over nearly a million years. P. robustus reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> by about 2 million years ago.</p><p>Paranthropus lived alongside early human species, among them Homo habilis and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homo-erectus">Homo erectus</a>, and was preyed on by crocodiles, leopards, sabre-toothed cats and hyenas. Explanations for its extinction include a drying trend from 1.45 million years ago, which pushed back woodland, and competition with savanna baboons and Homo for food.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Paranthropus had a massive, tall skull with a sagittal crest that anchored large chewing muscles, broad molars with thick enamel, and a braincase of about 500 cubic centimetres. Its body was small: P. robustus males are estimated at 40 kilograms and females at 32 kilograms. It walked upright. P. boisei, once nicknamed Nutcracker Man, probably preferred soft foods and ate hard ones only in lean times. P. robustus was probably an omnivore, while P. boisei seems to have been largely herbivorous.</p><p>Bone tools from the caves of Swartkrans, Kromdraai and Drimolen, dated between 2.3 and 0.6 million years ago, are often associated with P. robustus. A strontium isotope study of P. robustus teeth indicated that females were more likely to leave their place of birth.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paranthropus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
